910:
(a) All persons desiring a wrecker service vehicle operator's permit shall make application to the Snellville Police Department, Chief of Police or his/her designee on the form prescribed by the City of Snellville.
(b) The application shall include, but not be limited to, the name, age, date of birth and home address of the applicant, shall state whether he/she has ever been convicted of any offense specified in the operator's standard's section of this division, and if so, shall furnish a complete and detailed statement of the convictions.
(c) The applicant shall sign a release allowing for the chief of police or his designee to request a criminal background check from the Georgia Crime Information Center as well as a driver's history from the Georgia Department of Driver's Services.
(d) No permit shall be granted unless the applicant is at least 18 years of age and holds a commercial driver's license from the State of Georgia for the class of vehicle to be operated.
(e) No wrecker service vehicle operator's permit shall be issued if the applicant has been convicted of any of the following offenses:
(1) Any felony;
(2) Any crime of moral turpitude;
(3) Any misdemeanor crime of violence within ten years of application;
(4) Any misdemeanor crime involving the use of controlled substances; marijuana and intoxicants within ten years of application;
(5) Vehicular homicide;
(6) Serious injury by vehicle;
(7) Hit and run;
(8) Driving under the influence of alcohol and/or drugs within ten years of application;
(9) Plea of guilty, nolo contendre or forfeiture of bail, not vacated, upon three charges of violation of the motor vehicle laws of this state within a period of 12 months.
(f) All applications shall be sworn to by the applicant before a notary public or other officer empowered by law to administer oaths.
(g) The Snellville Chief of Police or their designee shall investigate each applicant for a wrecker service vehicle operator's permit and may consider extenuating circumstances which may reflect favorably or unfavorably upon the applicant. If in their judgment circumstances are such that granting of the permit would not be in the best interest of the general public, such circumstances may be grounds for denying the application.
(h) In all instances in which an application is denied under the provisions of this division, the applicant may not reapply for a permit for at least one year from the final date of such denial.
(i) Any wrecker service vehicle operator who is charged with committing any offense delineated in subsections (e)(1)—(8) of this section shall have his permit suspended until the case is resolved. Upon conviction, said permit will be revoked.
(j) Any wrecker service vehicle operator who is charged with committing any traffic offense or other crime not delineated in subsections (e)(1)—(8) of this section shall have his permit suspended until the case is resolved. Upon conviction, said permit may be suspended or revoked at the discretion of the chief of police or their designee.
(Ord. No. 2015-08, 6-8-2015)

